;;; tur/string-reader -- RETIRED no-op; `#s"..."` is now always available.
;;;
;;; `#s"text"` reads as `(string/from-cstr "text")` -- a fresh owned String --
;;; while bare `"text"` stays a borrowed `cstr`.  That dispatch used to be
;;; opt-in, through either this file or the `stringed` `#lang` layer.  It is
;;; now unconditional: the reader installs it for every file, whatever its
;;; base dialect, so neither directive is needed any more.
;;;
;;; What a program still needs is the code the expansion CALLS:
;;;
;;;   (load "stdlib/string.tur")   ;; EVAL time: string/from-cstr and friends
;;;
;;;   ... #s"hello" ...            ;; => (string/from-cstr "hello"), owned
;;;
;;; The read-time/eval-time split is why that `(load)` is still required and
;;; is genuinely non-obvious: `#s"..."` is expanded while the file is being
;;; READ, long before any `(load ...)` form runs, so the load cannot enable
;;; the syntax -- it only supplies the function the syntax expands into.
;;;
;;; This file is kept, empty of definitions, for one minor line so that a
;;; file in the wild carrying `#use-reader-macros "stdlib/string-reader.tur"`
;;; keeps compiling and its reader learns why.  Re-registering `#s"` here
;;; would be a hard "reader macro '#s' already registered" against the strict
;;; batch registry -- a working file turned into a compile error -- which is
;;; exactly what this no-op prevents.
;;;
;;; Delete at age-out (0.50.0); see
;;; docs/archive/lang-layers-decommission-plan.md.
;;;
;;; Since: v2 (owned-string-type-plan literal ergonomics); retired at 0.49.0
